[{:seon.task/name "a-backslash-starts-a-character-anywhere-in-a-token"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23: main red on seon.reader-test/the-laws-hold-over-generated-replies at seed 449380042768857075, the replies [\"`\" \"\\\\\" \"(\"], a latent reader fault a reseed exposed. The law that broke: forgiving forgiven text changes nothing. seon.reader/scan read the token `\\ as ending before the ( (a backslash started a character literal only at a token's start), so it closed a paren that `\\( never opened (syntax-quote of the character (), and the second pass, unable to read `\\(), made the line prose. Correct, at the producer: a backslash anywhere in a token starts a character literal that takes the next character whatever it is, and a closer is never inserted right after a backslash ending a line (lone), wherever in a token it stands. The counterexample is a fixed case of seon.reader-test/texts beside the generated ones."
  :seon.task/kind :work
